WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.302 --> 00:00:04.058
- I guess if you have an addiction in life

00:00:04.058 --> 00:00:06.116
it's nice to have one like curiosity.

00:00:06.116 --> 00:00:08.098
I am not going to figure
out where to drive the bus

00:00:08.098 --> 00:00:10.172
until I've got the
right people on the bus.

00:00:10.172 --> 00:00:13.074
Not only that, 15 of the
18 built to last companies

00:00:13.074 --> 00:00:15.315
lived through the 1930s depression.

00:00:15.315 --> 00:00:18.482
I can remain focused on and suffer for

00:00:19.978 --> 00:00:23.164
the big thing longer than anyone else.

00:00:23.164 --> 00:00:25.528
And I'm about to fall,

00:00:25.528 --> 00:00:27.573
I'm 400 feet above the ground.

00:00:27.573 --> 00:00:29.924
Please turn off your electronic gadgets.

00:00:29.924 --> 00:00:32.260
But what was Intel known for?

00:00:32.260 --> 00:00:33.764
Intel delivers.

00:00:33.764 --> 00:00:37.189
See, if you have the right people

00:00:37.189 --> 00:00:38.731
they're already motivated.

00:00:38.731 --> 00:00:41.229
The question is not risk or not risk,

00:00:41.229 --> 00:00:43.906
the question is where do
you place your big bets.

00:00:43.906 --> 00:00:45.831
I'm genetically encoded to do it,

00:00:45.831 --> 00:00:47.555
when I do it I feel like a fish in water.

00:00:47.555 --> 00:00:49.826
- He's an American business consultant,

00:00:49.826 --> 00:00:51.289
author, and lecturer.

00:00:51.289 --> 00:00:53.664
- He's a student and a
teacher of leadership.

00:00:53.664 --> 00:00:55.966
- He's authored or coauthored six books

00:00:55.966 --> 00:00:58.789
that have sold over 10
million copies worldwide.

00:00:58.789 --> 00:01:01.253
- He's Jim Collins, and here are his

00:01:01.253 --> 00:01:03.336
top 10 rules for success.

00:01:06.530 --> 00:01:09.701
- I guess if you have an addiction in life

00:01:09.701 --> 00:01:12.354
it's nice to have one like curiosity.

00:01:12.354 --> 00:01:16.161
I mean, questions for me
are where everything begins

00:01:16.161 --> 00:01:18.880
and always like to describe it that

00:01:18.880 --> 00:01:22.088
I don't pick questions,
in many ways they pick me.

00:01:22.088 --> 00:01:25.604
They kind of rise up and grab
around the throat and say

00:01:25.604 --> 00:01:27.469
I'm not going to let go.

00:01:27.469 --> 00:01:30.136
And to me life is just a journey

00:01:31.064 --> 00:01:33.918
of question upon question,
and there's so many chances

00:01:33.918 --> 00:01:35.284
to learn things.

00:01:35.284 --> 00:01:38.630
One of my mentors, John Gardner,

00:01:38.630 --> 00:01:40.943
who was in the Johnson administration,

00:01:40.943 --> 00:01:42.837
was a senior faculty at Stanford.

00:01:42.837 --> 00:01:43.738
- Wrote about excellence.

00:01:43.738 --> 00:01:45.469
- Wrote about excellence
truly before people

00:01:45.469 --> 00:01:48.061
were thinking about it, and
renewal, and self renewal,

00:01:48.061 --> 00:01:50.804
and John had this
wonderful way of saying it,

00:01:50.804 --> 00:01:53.563
don't try to be
interesting, be interested.

00:01:53.563 --> 00:01:56.103
And that stuck with me,

00:01:56.103 --> 00:01:58.047
and this idea of always be interested,

00:01:58.047 --> 00:01:59.836
and you never know where
a question will go,

00:01:59.836 --> 00:02:02.473
you sit down with somebody
and you ask a simple question

00:02:02.473 --> 00:02:05.094
like where are you from?

00:02:05.094 --> 00:02:06.964
So think about the power of that question,

00:02:06.964 --> 00:02:08.249
where are you from?

00:02:08.249 --> 00:02:09.616
That's not a hierarchical question,

00:02:09.616 --> 00:02:12.659
that's not a question of what do you do

00:02:12.659 --> 00:02:15.628
or how important are you,
it's where are you from?

00:02:15.628 --> 00:02:17.855
And somebody can choose,
it's an invitation,

00:02:17.855 --> 00:02:21.750
well, I'm from Virginia,
or I'm from Greece,

00:02:21.750 --> 00:02:25.073
or I'm from, they can name
their company, they can choose,

00:02:25.073 --> 00:02:27.174
and the interesting
thing is you never know

00:02:27.174 --> 00:02:28.415
where that will go.

00:02:28.415 --> 00:02:32.214
And then in actual work it all
has to begin with questions

00:02:32.214 --> 00:02:35.522
because if you actually know the answers

00:02:35.522 --> 00:02:38.136
then I don't understand the
point of doing the work.

00:02:38.136 --> 00:02:40.700
When Dick Cooley became chief
executive of Wells Fargo

00:02:40.700 --> 00:02:43.604
in the late 1970s he
knew he would be facing

00:02:43.604 --> 00:02:46.223
the storm of deregulation,
he knew that the entire

00:02:46.223 --> 00:02:48.448
banking industry would be upended

00:02:48.448 --> 00:02:51.369
when deregulation swept through.

00:02:51.369 --> 00:02:53.785
The board, understandably concerned,

00:02:53.785 --> 00:02:55.704
asked Cooley, what is your vision,

00:02:55.704 --> 00:02:57.890
what is your strategy,
where will you lead us,

00:02:57.890 --> 00:03:01.455
how will you get us through
this storm of deregulation?

00:03:01.455 --> 00:03:04.030
And Cooley had a wonderful answer,

00:03:04.030 --> 00:03:05.113
I don't know.

00:03:06.803 --> 00:03:10.486
Not only that, it's the wrong question.

00:03:10.486 --> 00:03:13.728
See, I am not going to first figure out

00:03:13.728 --> 00:03:18.151
where to drive this bus and
then get people on the bus.

00:03:18.151 --> 00:03:20.709
No, I'm going to do it
completely opposite.

00:03:20.709 --> 00:03:22.729
I am not going to figure
out where to drive the bus

00:03:22.729 --> 00:03:25.525
until I've got the
right people on the bus.

00:03:25.525 --> 00:03:28.621
And only once I've got the
right people on the bus,

00:03:28.621 --> 00:03:30.440
the wrong people off the bus,

00:03:30.440 --> 00:03:32.838
and the right people on the key seats,

00:03:32.838 --> 00:03:37.339
then and only then will I turn
my attention to the question

00:03:37.339 --> 00:03:40.006
of where we will drive this bus.

00:03:41.999 --> 00:03:44.749
The best executives we've studied

00:03:45.658 --> 00:03:47.991
always think first about who

00:03:49.473 --> 00:03:51.460
and then about what.

00:03:51.460 --> 00:03:53.760
It's not about just getting a great team,

00:03:53.760 --> 00:03:55.477
it's not about finding talent,

00:03:55.477 --> 00:03:58.569
it's not about getting great people, no.

00:03:58.569 --> 00:04:00.933
The key word is first.

00:04:00.933 --> 00:04:02.895
First get right people,

00:04:02.895 --> 00:04:04.591
first get the people on the bus,

00:04:04.591 --> 00:04:06.609
first think about who,

00:04:06.609 --> 00:04:10.111
put who up ahead of strategy,
who ahead of tactics,

00:04:10.111 --> 00:04:13.208
who ahead of technology,
who ahead of business ideas,

00:04:13.208 --> 00:04:15.207
who ahead of everything.

00:04:15.207 --> 00:04:16.957
First who, first who,

00:04:18.372 --> 00:04:20.122
first who, then what.

00:04:21.451 --> 00:04:23.813
I mentioned earlier
the work built to last.

00:04:23.813 --> 00:04:25.273
It was very interesting,
we were studying enduring

00:04:25.273 --> 00:04:28.223
great companies in contrast to others,

00:04:28.223 --> 00:04:29.877
went back recently and realized

00:04:29.877 --> 00:04:33.342
we selected the study set
for that study in 1989,

00:04:33.342 --> 00:04:35.207
two decades ago.

00:04:35.207 --> 00:04:38.259
All 18 of the built to last companies

00:04:38.259 --> 00:04:42.483
are still standalone, independent,
and almost all of them

00:04:42.483 --> 00:04:45.168
very successful companies today.

00:04:45.168 --> 00:04:47.791
If you took a random sample
of large publicly traded

00:04:47.791 --> 00:04:49.708
companies 20 years ago,

00:04:50.631 --> 00:04:53.255
what are the probabilities
that all 18 in your random

00:04:53.255 --> 00:04:55.694
sample would be standalone,
independent, and largely

00:04:55.694 --> 00:04:57.914
successful today, the number is less,

00:04:57.914 --> 00:05:02.081
it's a percentage, is
about .02% probability.

00:05:03.744 --> 00:05:06.906
Not only that, 15 of the
18 built to last companies

00:05:06.906 --> 00:05:09.823
lived through the 1930s depression.

00:05:13.511 --> 00:05:15.976
What did they teach us?

00:05:15.976 --> 00:05:17.686
What has enabled them to have that?

00:05:17.686 --> 00:05:19.422
What did we find that separated them?

00:05:19.422 --> 00:05:22.583
And what we found is that
what really separated them

00:05:22.583 --> 00:05:24.604
was not necessarily that
they had smarter strategies,

00:05:24.604 --> 00:05:26.604
although they often did,

00:05:27.562 --> 00:05:29.807
or that they were sort of
more financially savvy,

00:05:29.807 --> 00:05:31.890
although they often were.

00:05:35.100 --> 00:05:37.932
It was because they were
founded first and foremost

00:05:37.932 --> 00:05:42.407
and built always on a rock
solid set of core values

00:05:42.407 --> 00:05:45.240
that are not open for negotiation.

00:05:47.067 --> 00:05:50.241
And if you look at what gave
them the reason to struggle,

00:05:50.241 --> 00:05:53.741
the reason to fight, the reason to endure,

00:05:55.946 --> 00:05:58.740
it wasn't strategic, it was values.

00:05:58.740 --> 00:06:01.128
One of the greatest, probably
the greatest rock climber

00:06:01.128 --> 00:06:03.316
of his generation, as you
know I've been a climber

00:06:03.316 --> 00:06:05.198
most of my life, is actually right now

00:06:05.198 --> 00:06:07.851
on the side of El Capitan
doing what will probably be

00:06:07.851 --> 00:06:11.886
the hardest rock climb certainly
ever done in our lifetimes,

00:06:11.886 --> 00:06:14.188
trying to free climb a
thing called the Don Wall,

00:06:14.188 --> 00:06:16.860
Mesolito side of El Cap,
young man named Tommy Caldwell

00:06:16.860 --> 00:06:20.413
who is, he's in a category of one.

00:06:20.413 --> 00:06:24.251
And I was out climbing with
Tommy one day and I said to him,

00:06:24.251 --> 00:06:25.722
Tommy, what makes you different?

00:06:25.722 --> 00:06:27.423
He has a record, he has
six climbs on El Cap

00:06:27.423 --> 00:06:29.441
that have never been repeated.

00:06:29.441 --> 00:06:33.730
He's just, he is 10x
in extreme environments

00:06:33.730 --> 00:06:35.499
and he's still alive.

00:06:35.499 --> 00:06:37.385
I said, Tommy, what is it that you have,

00:06:37.385 --> 00:06:39.387
you're not necessarily
more physically gifted,

00:06:39.387 --> 00:06:41.282
you're not necessarily stronger,

00:06:41.282 --> 00:06:43.205
you're certainly physically
gifted and all those things

00:06:43.205 --> 00:06:45.824
but you're not this incredible athlete

00:06:45.824 --> 00:06:48.498
that just was born this way.

00:06:48.498 --> 00:06:50.337
And in fact he'd lost a
finger in an accident,

00:06:50.337 --> 00:06:52.559
he cut a finger off,
and so he's doing this

00:06:52.559 --> 00:06:54.276
minus one of his.

00:06:54.276 --> 00:06:57.066
- An important finger?
- An important finger.

00:06:57.066 --> 00:07:00.769
And he thought about it
for one moment and he said,

00:07:00.769 --> 00:07:03.936
I can remain focused on and suffer for

00:07:05.509 --> 00:07:08.676
the big thing longer than anyone else.

00:07:10.581 --> 00:07:12.620
And I think that's what they do,

00:07:12.620 --> 00:07:14.317
the curiosity keeps them
going but once they get their

00:07:14.317 --> 00:07:19.252
hands on what they see is
the thing they don't let go

00:07:19.252 --> 00:07:22.124
and they can stay with
it and suffer for it.

00:07:22.124 --> 00:07:25.306
When I was in high school I must've been

00:07:25.306 --> 00:07:27.306
maybe 16 or 17 years old

00:07:28.900 --> 00:07:33.074
and I went rock climbing
in El Dorado Canyon

00:07:33.074 --> 00:07:35.187
near where I grew up in Boulder.

00:07:35.187 --> 00:07:36.978
I've been climbing about
three or four years

00:07:36.978 --> 00:07:39.483
and went to do a climb called T2.

00:07:39.483 --> 00:07:43.220
Now at the time, T2 is a
relatively difficult climb

00:07:43.220 --> 00:07:47.387
and I went at it with a kind
of arrogant lack of preparation

00:07:49.055 --> 00:07:51.638
and I hadn't prepared for being able to do

00:07:51.638 --> 00:07:53.171
certain kinds of finger cracks,

00:07:53.171 --> 00:07:55.713
I figured I'll figure
it out when I get there,

00:07:55.713 --> 00:07:58.887
and I also didn't pause,
and this is that thing

00:07:58.887 --> 00:08:01.197
about boldness and
discipline, I didn't pause

00:08:01.197 --> 00:08:04.364
to really double check all my systems.

00:08:06.430 --> 00:08:08.403
So I'm on the fourth pitch,

00:08:08.403 --> 00:08:11.403
I'm about 400 feet above the ground,

00:08:12.506 --> 00:08:16.113
I'm going across this
slanting crack system

00:08:16.113 --> 00:08:18.640
that I started to have trouble with

00:08:18.640 --> 00:08:20.936
and by forearm started
to engorge with blood

00:08:20.936 --> 00:08:23.126
and lactic acid, which means at some point

00:08:23.126 --> 00:08:27.802
even if you're on a big hold
your hands are going to melt off.

00:08:27.802 --> 00:08:30.206
And I'm about to fall,

00:08:30.206 --> 00:08:32.990
I'm 400 feet above the ground,

00:08:32.990 --> 00:08:35.815
but it should be okay
because there's protection,

00:08:35.815 --> 00:08:37.573
I'm tied into the rope, I may take a fall

00:08:37.573 --> 00:08:39.575
but I'll get caught.

00:08:39.575 --> 00:08:43.407
For some reason, and to this
day I still don't know why,

00:08:43.407 --> 00:08:48.308
my brain triggered that
I should look at my knot.

00:08:48.308 --> 00:08:51.121
And I looked down at my knot,

00:08:51.121 --> 00:08:53.121
my knot had come untied.

00:08:55.003 --> 00:08:58.633
I had made the mistake of
never really thinking about

00:08:58.633 --> 00:09:00.897
the best knot for certain
types of situations

00:09:00.897 --> 00:09:02.795
and I tied it with what's a bowline.

00:09:02.795 --> 00:09:06.222
Now the advantage of a
bowline is it's easy to untie,

00:09:06.222 --> 00:09:10.389
the disadvantage of a bowline
is it's easy to untie.

00:09:11.444 --> 00:09:15.374
It had come untied as I kept moving across

00:09:15.374 --> 00:09:19.164
and it it just hanging in my harness.

00:09:19.164 --> 00:09:23.331
I'm seconds away from
falling, only now I will die.

00:09:24.640 --> 00:09:26.723
I will die if I fall off.

00:09:27.711 --> 00:09:29.628
My forearms are melting

00:09:30.753 --> 00:09:33.464
and the knot had come
untied, and so I call down

00:09:33.464 --> 00:09:35.161
to my partner and I said,
my knot came untied,

00:09:35.161 --> 00:09:36.404
what is he going to do,
he can't do anything,

00:09:36.404 --> 00:09:38.434
he'll just watch me fall to my death.

00:09:38.434 --> 00:09:42.101
And there was an old
fixed piton in the rock

00:09:42.955 --> 00:09:44.718
that was put there from the first descent,

00:09:44.718 --> 00:09:47.188
an old soft iron piton,
and I looked at it,

00:09:47.188 --> 00:09:49.562
and I probably had 15, 20
seconds before my hands

00:09:49.562 --> 00:09:52.638
would unwrap, if that, maybe 10.

00:09:52.638 --> 00:09:55.944
I took a runner and I
clipped it into the piton

00:09:55.944 --> 00:09:59.978
and I clipped that runner into my harness

00:09:59.978 --> 00:10:03.395
and just went, please hold, and I let go.

00:10:05.708 --> 00:10:06.708
And it held.

00:10:07.595 --> 00:10:11.096
I put in a backup, I
grabbed the knot, retied it,

00:10:11.096 --> 00:10:12.924
went to the ledge, I didn't convulse,

00:10:12.924 --> 00:10:15.592
I was very calm at that
moment, when I got to the ledge

00:10:15.592 --> 00:10:18.086
I went into convulsive shaking,
I was sick to my stomach

00:10:18.086 --> 00:10:22.319
for days because of
what happens chemically.

00:10:22.319 --> 00:10:25.569
What I learned from that is two things.

00:10:26.747 --> 00:10:30.260
The first is that when you're
in an unforgiving world,

00:10:30.260 --> 00:10:33.656
gravity is unforgiving, it doesn't care,

00:10:33.656 --> 00:10:36.239
it never takes a day off, ever,

00:10:37.182 --> 00:10:39.813
and if you make one
mistake it can kill you

00:10:39.813 --> 00:10:41.388
if it's the wrong kind of mistake,

00:10:41.388 --> 00:10:42.991
and what I learned is that
sense of, yeah, I want to do

00:10:42.991 --> 00:10:44.848
adventurous things, I want to climb rocks,

00:10:44.848 --> 00:10:46.574
I want to be in Yosemite,
I want to do things

00:10:46.574 --> 00:10:50.582
that get my adrenaline going,
but I need the discipline

00:10:50.582 --> 00:10:53.172
to always do all the right preparation,

00:10:53.172 --> 00:10:54.974
to always think about the right knots,

00:10:54.974 --> 00:10:58.210
to always be cross checking
my system so that I can

00:10:58.210 --> 00:11:00.706
do those bold things and stay alive

00:11:00.706 --> 00:11:03.413
in an unforgiving environment,
and as you and I spoke about

00:11:03.413 --> 00:11:05.628
earlier today, I think that's the recipe,

00:11:05.628 --> 00:11:08.792
I think we are all heading
into an unforgiving environment

00:11:08.792 --> 00:11:11.994
where we're going to have to
be both bold and disciplined.

00:11:11.994 --> 00:11:14.407
The second thing I learned,

00:11:14.407 --> 00:11:17.877
what if that piton had not been there?

00:11:17.877 --> 00:11:20.735
We might not be here together.

00:11:20.735 --> 00:11:24.902
It was just by chance that
that piton was right there.

00:11:27.056 --> 00:11:29.118
And what you realize
when you rewind the tape

00:11:29.118 --> 00:11:32.680
of your own life is that there
are these crucial moments

00:11:32.680 --> 00:11:36.013
where if the luck had gone the other way

00:11:36.927 --> 00:11:39.423
you might have gotten killed.

00:11:39.423 --> 00:11:42.015
And so what you have to do is to always be

00:11:42.015 --> 00:11:44.856
putting those extra discipline in place

00:11:44.856 --> 00:11:47.189
because I was lucky to live,

00:11:48.323 --> 00:11:51.565
and what I took from that
is I never want to have

00:11:51.565 --> 00:11:53.732
to be lucky to live again.

00:11:55.026 --> 00:11:57.771
I want to live because of my discipline

00:11:57.771 --> 00:11:59.485
not because of my luck.

00:11:59.485 --> 00:12:02.818
Please turn off your electronic gadgets.

00:12:05.597 --> 00:12:08.264
Not for others but for yourself.

00:12:10.125 --> 00:12:13.125
Effective people take time to think.

00:12:15.466 --> 00:12:18.336
Begin the discipline
of putting white space

00:12:18.336 --> 00:12:21.953
on your calendar where
there's no phone, no email,

00:12:21.953 --> 00:12:23.832
I was going to say no fax
but they don't even have that

00:12:23.832 --> 00:12:28.187
anymore, no Twitter, no
emails, no connections,

00:12:28.187 --> 00:12:31.450
and engage in this
glorious pockets of quiet.

00:12:31.450 --> 00:12:35.034
Do you know that Rick Warden
reads a book every single day?

00:12:35.034 --> 00:12:36.117
A book a day.

00:12:39.444 --> 00:12:41.944
A book a day, 365 days a year.

00:12:43.493 --> 00:12:46.445
You're at a thousand books in three years.

00:12:46.445 --> 00:12:48.888
First, innovation is extremely important

00:12:48.888 --> 00:12:51.832
and I would not want
to send any indication,

00:12:51.832 --> 00:12:54.117
we should stop innovating and
America shouldn't innovate,

00:12:54.117 --> 00:12:55.867
that'd be inaccurate.

00:12:57.288 --> 00:12:58.521
- Well, we better damn well
understand what made us

00:12:58.521 --> 00:13:01.156
innovative and make sure
we don't lose those things.

00:13:01.156 --> 00:13:03.583
- Exactly right, we can't
afford to lose those things.

00:13:03.583 --> 00:13:07.540
What we found is that any
given industry has a threshold

00:13:07.540 --> 00:13:10.813
level of innovation and
that once you get to that,

00:13:10.813 --> 00:13:12.414
you have to get to that threshold.

00:13:12.414 --> 00:13:15.098
For something like airlines
it's relatively low

00:13:15.098 --> 00:13:17.787
and for something like
biotech it's really high.

00:13:17.787 --> 00:13:20.632
But once you meet that
threshold of innovation

00:13:20.632 --> 00:13:23.960
it's not innovation alone
that makes you exceptional,

00:13:23.960 --> 00:13:27.179
it is actually the blend
of being able to put

00:13:27.179 --> 00:13:28.981
the innovation plus the
organizational discipline.

00:13:28.981 --> 00:13:32.393
Again, we go back to was Apple
just an innovation story?

00:13:32.393 --> 00:13:34.376
No, at some point it was a
whole bunch of other things

00:13:34.376 --> 00:13:35.845
around it that made it work.

00:13:35.845 --> 00:13:37.390
If we go back and we look at Intel,

00:13:37.390 --> 00:13:39.762
what was Intel known for?

00:13:39.762 --> 00:13:42.348
Well, yes, it had a lot of
innovation because to be

00:13:42.348 --> 00:13:44.280
in semiconductor industry
you had to have a lot

00:13:44.280 --> 00:13:47.130
of innovation, but were they
always the most innovative, no.

00:13:47.130 --> 00:13:48.995
And at critical junctures
they might even have been

00:13:48.995 --> 00:13:51.989
a little bit behind, but
what was Intel known for?

00:13:51.989 --> 00:13:53.968
Intel delivers.

00:13:53.968 --> 00:13:57.760
So, you are IBM, what's
your worst nightmare?

00:13:57.760 --> 00:13:59.632
You don't have enough
chips at affordable cost

00:13:59.632 --> 00:14:02.453
when the time comes, and you
can look at different kinds,

00:14:02.453 --> 00:14:03.894
you got maybe one chip this
morning that's innovative

00:14:03.894 --> 00:14:06.306
but you've got another chip
that's innovative enough

00:14:06.306 --> 00:14:08.507
but it's got the organization
behind it with the discipline

00:14:08.507 --> 00:14:12.094
and it delivers, where
are you going to bet?

00:14:12.094 --> 00:14:15.116
- It delivers.
- It delivers, exactly right.

00:14:15.116 --> 00:14:18.095
I've always loved the
story Silver Blaze which is

00:14:18.095 --> 00:14:21.004
the Sherlock Holmes story
where at the end of the case

00:14:21.004 --> 00:14:24.158
the constable asks Holmes,
what was the key to the case,

00:14:24.158 --> 00:14:25.955
and Holmes says, it was
the curious incident

00:14:25.955 --> 00:14:27.805
of the dog in the nighttime.

00:14:27.805 --> 00:14:29.498
And the constable says, but
the dog didn't do anything

00:14:29.498 --> 00:14:30.599
in the nighttime.

00:14:30.599 --> 00:14:34.017
Ah ha, yes, that was the curious incident,

00:14:34.017 --> 00:14:37.902
the dog didn't bark, and so
therefore I knew the criminal

00:14:37.902 --> 00:14:41.952
must've been somebody who knew the dog.

00:14:41.952 --> 00:14:43.202
In our research

00:14:44.558 --> 00:14:47.326
it is the dogs that do not bark

00:14:47.326 --> 00:14:50.407
that often give us some of the best clues.

00:14:50.407 --> 00:14:53.204
And the dogs that do
not bark helped us see

00:14:53.204 --> 00:14:55.315
the who principle.

00:14:55.315 --> 00:14:57.250
Now you would think that
if you're leading a company

00:14:57.250 --> 00:15:00.332
from good to great, one of
the things you would expect

00:15:00.332 --> 00:15:02.804
to see, one of the dogs
you would expect to bark,

00:15:02.804 --> 00:15:06.071
is that you would find very
motivating and charismatic

00:15:06.071 --> 00:15:08.977
leaders who could infuse
motivation into the troops

00:15:08.977 --> 00:15:12.906
so that they would go out
and do good to great things.

00:15:12.906 --> 00:15:16.028
Well, as our research
unfolded what we found is that

00:15:16.028 --> 00:15:18.420
most of the good to great leaders had had

00:15:18.420 --> 00:15:19.908
a charisma bypass.

00:15:19.908 --> 00:15:21.678
They couldn't motivate
anybody out of just a

00:15:21.678 --> 00:15:25.761
pure motivational force,
that's not how they led.

00:15:26.691 --> 00:15:28.803
But then when we began asking them,

00:15:28.803 --> 00:15:30.653
how did you get alignment, how did you get

00:15:30.653 --> 00:15:34.417
people motivated behind
what you were trying to do?

00:15:34.417 --> 00:15:37.139
The dog just didn't bark.

00:15:37.139 --> 00:15:39.436
They didn't spend any time on that.

00:15:39.436 --> 00:15:41.712
That's not how they led.

00:15:41.712 --> 00:15:43.237
And we scratched our heads a little bit,

00:15:43.237 --> 00:15:44.810
how would you make sense of the fact that

00:15:44.810 --> 00:15:46.970
you're getting a whole
company to go in a different

00:15:46.970 --> 00:15:49.453
direction and yet the
leaders who made this happen

00:15:49.453 --> 00:15:52.680
didn't invest in figuring
out how to motivate people,

00:15:52.680 --> 00:15:54.980
how to inspire people, how to get people

00:15:54.980 --> 00:15:56.647
behind the flywheel.

00:15:58.917 --> 00:16:01.936
And this is where we learned something.

00:16:01.936 --> 00:16:05.447
See, if you have the right people

00:16:05.447 --> 00:16:08.131
they're already motivated.

00:16:08.131 --> 00:16:10.877
The right people are self motivated.

00:16:10.877 --> 00:16:14.044
The right people are self disciplined.

00:16:15.534 --> 00:16:18.847
And the challenge of management,
the challenge of leadership

00:16:18.847 --> 00:16:23.146
is not how to figure out how
to motivate the wrong people

00:16:23.146 --> 00:16:25.714
into the right people,

00:16:25.714 --> 00:16:28.270
it's how to get the right
people and then not do

00:16:28.270 --> 00:16:31.501
all the stupid management
things that tend to

00:16:31.501 --> 00:16:34.834
demotivate the already motivated people.

00:16:36.056 --> 00:16:38.634
- So what you also talked
about in your book is

00:16:38.634 --> 00:16:41.891
not to take big risks, not to,
you said, fire cannonballs.

00:16:41.891 --> 00:16:43.736
- Yeah, well you want to fire cannonballs,

00:16:43.736 --> 00:16:46.101
so this is the really interesting thing,

00:16:46.101 --> 00:16:47.863
the fire bullets then fire cannonballs,

00:16:47.863 --> 00:16:51.622
it's not a matter of, the
question is not risk or not risk,

00:16:51.622 --> 00:16:54.545
the question is where do
you place your big bets

00:16:54.545 --> 00:16:56.845
cause if you don't place a big bet

00:16:56.845 --> 00:16:58.716
then you're never
actually going to achieve

00:16:58.716 --> 00:17:01.603
exceptional results, if
you're never really going big.

00:17:01.603 --> 00:17:04.833
The question is how do
you place your big bets

00:17:04.833 --> 00:17:07.166
and bound your risk at the same time,

00:17:07.166 --> 00:17:09.276
and there the answer is,
what we found is that

00:17:09.276 --> 00:17:10.957
all of our companies
and all of our leaders

00:17:10.957 --> 00:17:14.491
did place big bets but
they placed them after

00:17:14.491 --> 00:17:17.229
they had proven that they had
taken most of the risk out

00:17:17.229 --> 00:17:19.816
by proving that that bet
would likely pay off.

00:17:19.816 --> 00:17:22.434
The uncalibrated cannonball is dangerous,

00:17:22.434 --> 00:17:25.390
the calibrated cannonball is
how you get something great.

00:17:25.390 --> 00:17:30.206
If you think about sort of
how people apply themselves,

00:17:30.206 --> 00:17:33.090
when we go back and we look
at the good to great data

00:17:33.090 --> 00:17:34.857
and some of the other data, we find that

00:17:34.857 --> 00:17:37.573
there's these three circles
and you put your energies

00:17:37.573 --> 00:17:38.924
in the middle of three circles.

00:17:38.924 --> 00:17:41.683
And the first circle is
what you're passionate about

00:17:41.683 --> 00:17:45.217
and what you love to do
and what you stand for.

00:17:45.217 --> 00:17:47.574
And the second circle is
what you can be the best at,

00:17:47.574 --> 00:17:50.141
and the third circle is what
drives your economic engine.

00:17:50.141 --> 00:17:52.004
And you focus your energies there,

00:17:52.004 --> 00:17:55.685
but let's drop that down
a level to the individual.

00:17:55.685 --> 00:17:58.571
How many of the folks under age 30 in here

00:17:58.571 --> 00:18:01.020
have had cross your mind the thought,

00:18:01.020 --> 00:18:03.778
I wonder what I'm going to do with myself.

00:18:03.778 --> 00:18:05.092
(laughter)

00:18:05.092 --> 00:18:08.427
Okay, I'd like you to think then about

00:18:08.427 --> 00:18:12.280
finding your own three circles,
at an individual level,

00:18:12.280 --> 00:18:14.054
which is think about it this way,

00:18:14.054 --> 00:18:18.221
imagine that you could engage
your energies and your time

00:18:20.381 --> 00:18:22.579
directly in the middle of three tess.

00:18:22.579 --> 00:18:26.746
First, it is something for
which you have great passion,

00:18:28.088 --> 00:18:31.722
that you love to do, and
that absolutely reflects

00:18:31.722 --> 00:18:33.494
your values, and when you
wake up in the morning

00:18:33.494 --> 00:18:35.864
there's this sense of,
my goodness, even if

00:18:35.864 --> 00:18:38.819
I'm getting paid for this,
I would want to do it

00:18:38.819 --> 00:18:41.902
even if I wasn't getting paid for it.

00:18:42.743 --> 00:18:44.707
Now imagine if in addition to that

00:18:44.707 --> 00:18:48.221
you could marry it to a second circle

00:18:48.221 --> 00:18:52.880
which is finding what you're
genetically encoded for.

00:18:52.880 --> 00:18:56.075
And there's a big difference
of what you're good at

00:18:56.075 --> 00:18:59.413
and what you're genetically encoded for.

00:18:59.413 --> 00:19:02.129
I discovered this as a young person.

00:19:02.129 --> 00:19:03.869
I went off to college,
I thought I would be

00:19:03.869 --> 00:19:07.149
a mathematician, I had
done well on math tests,

00:19:07.149 --> 00:19:10.315
but when I entered
courses like Real Analysis

00:19:10.315 --> 00:19:14.094
I met those who were
genetically encoded for math.

00:19:14.094 --> 00:19:16.344
(laughter)

00:19:20.001 --> 00:19:23.024
Not being one of them I
needed to find another version

00:19:23.024 --> 00:19:25.072
of my three circles.

00:19:25.072 --> 00:19:26.876
(laughter)

00:19:26.876 --> 00:19:29.626
And now imagine the third circle.

00:19:31.270 --> 00:19:33.770
As you're engaged in something

00:19:36.303 --> 00:19:39.220
that makes, that is of great value.

00:19:42.478 --> 00:19:46.031
It's of either social or
economic or both of value,

00:19:46.031 --> 00:19:49.364
it makes a contribution, you are useful.

00:19:51.154 --> 00:19:53.737
Now imagine you have all three.

00:19:54.599 --> 00:19:56.398
Man, I'm passionate about
this, I love to do it,

00:19:56.398 --> 00:19:59.582
it expresses my values, I'm
genetically encoded to do it,

00:19:59.582 --> 00:20:01.656
when I do it I feel like a fish in water,

00:20:01.656 --> 00:20:04.656
and then finally, third, I'm useful.

00:20:07.525 --> 00:20:11.692
What percentage of the
world do you think has that?

00:20:14.198 --> 00:20:15.281
Five percent?

00:20:17.081 --> 00:20:18.933
Maybe not even.

00:20:18.933 --> 00:20:21.524
What would happen to the world if,

00:20:21.524 --> 00:20:24.565
let's say it's three percent,

00:20:24.565 --> 00:20:26.815
if we then made it 20%

00:20:28.131 --> 00:20:29.981
of people who are doing what
they're passionate about,

00:20:29.981 --> 00:20:33.106
genetically encoded for, and are useful,

00:20:33.106 --> 00:20:37.106
are in positions of real
contribution and value.

00:20:40.081 --> 00:20:42.920
Now I don't know the answer
of how we make that percentage

00:20:42.920 --> 00:20:46.546
go up, but linking back
to the idea of Maslow,

00:20:46.546 --> 00:20:49.301
how did he describe self actualization?

00:20:49.301 --> 00:20:52.047
It wasn't hanging out on the beach.

00:20:52.047 --> 00:20:53.728
(laughter)

00:20:53.728 --> 00:20:56.707
He defined self
actualization as discovering

00:20:56.707 --> 00:20:58.790
what you were meant to do

00:21:01.000 --> 00:21:05.167
and committing to the ardor of
pursuing it with excellence.

00:21:08.527 --> 00:21:12.124
The purpose of free
society, I would suggest,

00:21:12.124 --> 00:21:15.540
is to systematically increase
the percentage of people

00:21:15.540 --> 00:21:17.207
who do exactly that.

00:21:23.782 --> 00:21:28.245
And then they can do it for
very long periods of time.

00:21:28.245 --> 00:21:29.526
- Thank you so much for watching.

00:21:29.526 --> 00:21:32.599
- We made this video
because Arnos asked us to.

00:21:32.599 --> 00:21:35.099
If there is anyone that you
would like us to feature

00:21:35.099 --> 00:21:36.478
please leave a comment below.

00:21:36.478 --> 00:21:38.105
- We'd also love to know
which of Jim Collins's

00:21:38.105 --> 00:21:40.044
top 10 rules meant the most to you,

00:21:40.044 --> 00:21:41.302
leave it in the comments below

00:21:41.302 --> 00:21:42.738
and we'll join the discussion.

00:21:42.738 --> 00:21:44.217
Thank you so much for watching,

00:21:44.217 --> 00:21:47.884
continue to believe,
and we'll see you soon.

